    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Can you lose weight with an under desk elliptical? Yes, but the effect is gradual, and it depends far more on your total daily habits than on the machine. Pedaling turns sitting time into light activity, which adds a steady trickle of extra calories burned. Over weeks and months that can support weight loss, as long as you do not eat it back.</p>
<p>This guide runs the realistic numbers by body weight, explains why the classic "3,500 calories per pound" rule is only a rough guide, and lays out the diet and consistency habits that decide whether you actually see results.</p>

<h2>Can You Lose Weight With an Under Desk Elliptical? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>Weight loss comes down to burning more energy than you take in over time. An under desk elliptical helps on the "burning more" side without taking extra time out of your day. That is its real strength: you can pedal for an hour or two during work you would be doing anyway.</p>
<p>The limit is size. Light to moderate seated pedaling adds roughly 50 to 150 calories per hour above what you burn sitting still, depending on your weight and effort. That is meaningful when you do it daily, but it is small compared with what you eat. A single latte or a handful of chips can match an hour of pedaling.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>The honest version:</strong> an under desk elliptical is a good weight-loss <em>helper</em>. It is not a weight-loss <em>plan</em>. Results come when you pair it with a modest calorie deficit from food.</p></div>

<h2>The Weight Loss Math: Extra Calories vs Sitting</h2>
<p>To estimate fat loss, count only the calories above sitting. Sitting at a desk is about 1.3 METs in the Compendium of Physical Activities. Light seated pedaling is about 2.0 METs and moderate pedaling about 2.8. The difference, multiplied by your body weight, is your extra burn.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
