<h2>Who Will See the Best Results?</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Heavy sitters:</strong> if you sit 7 or more hours a day, you have the most idle time to convert into movement.</li>
<li><strong>People who struggle to find gym time:</strong> pedaling fits into your workday with no commute or changing clothes.</li>
<li><strong>Beginners and older adults:</strong> low-impact motion is a gentle way to start. See the <a href="/best-under-desk-elliptical-for-seniors/">best under desk elliptical for seniors</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Less ideal:</strong> people who already exercise hard most days will see a smaller relative effect, though it still helps on rest days.</li>
<li><strong>Standing desk users:</strong> if you would rather stand, a walking pad burns more per hour. Compare the two in <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-vs-walking-pad/">under desk elliptical vs walking pad</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Verdict: Is an Under Desk Elliptical Good for Weight Loss?</h2>
<p>Yes, as a steady helper. Used for an hour or two a day, it can add around 1,000 or more extra calories a week for many people, which may support roughly 1 to 2 pounds of fat loss a month when your diet stays in check. It works because it fits into time you already spend sitting, not because it is intense.</p>
